WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.640
Imagine your entire life, every complicated decision, every messy argument you've ever had,

00:06.800 --> 00:14.660
every ambiguous moment. All of it. Yeah, all of it. Just boiled down to exactly 11 tidy lines in

00:14.660 --> 00:18.940
a dictionary. I mean, what gets lost in that compression? Basically everything, right? Right.

00:19.180 --> 00:24.100
Welcome to the Deep Dive. Today, we're unpacking a really fascinating experimental screenplay

00:24.100 --> 00:30.740
called City of Brutes by an independent researcher known as Flyxion. And it's not really your standard

00:30.740 --> 00:35.920
narrative. It functions much more like this dense work of institutional philosophy that's just

00:35.920 --> 00:42.140
disguised as cinema. Exactly. It's this profound exploration of how institutions process information

00:42.140 --> 00:47.180
and how, you know, reality just gets flattened into history. Yeah. And it operates around this

00:47.180 --> 00:53.160
really startling hypothesis from the 16th century. It goes, well, roughly translated. It's that brute

00:53.160 --> 00:58.600
animals employ reason better than man. Which is such a deliberate provocation, you know? Yeah.

00:58.720 --> 01:03.300
Because we're taught that human language, our ability to map and document things, is our ultimate

01:03.300 --> 01:09.840
evolutionary advantage. Right. But this text suggests the exact opposite. It argues that our institutions,

01:10.220 --> 01:15.560
our endless paperwork, our desperate need to represent the world before we interact with it.

01:15.620 --> 01:20.380
It actually cryptos us. Exactly. It completely paralyzes us. So to really get into this,

01:20.380 --> 01:25.140
we're going to explore three recurring domains from the text. We've got the administrative domain,

01:25.300 --> 01:29.900
which is how humans try to create order out of noise. Then there's the animal domain,

01:30.260 --> 01:36.140
where creatures navigate the exact same world through pure, unmediated action. And finally,

01:36.220 --> 01:41.640
the performative domain, right? Where human roles and identities just totally detach from reality.

01:42.020 --> 01:45.860
What's fascinating here is that Flyxion describes this whole screenplay

01:45.860 --> 01:50.880
as an accumulation of impossibility. Oh, I love that phrase. Right. It shows a system

01:50.880 --> 01:55.780
functioning perfectly, operating at absolute maximum capacity, while simultaneously making

01:55.780 --> 02:01.880
just zero sense globally. I mean, think about how you process the endless flood of information in

02:01.880 --> 02:07.200
your own life. Your emails, your meetings, those massive group chats. Are you actually understanding

02:07.200 --> 02:11.700
it or are you just functioning within it? Yeah, we're really watching the triumph of procedure

02:11.700 --> 02:17.800
over truth here. So the text throws us right into this dynamic. The opening scene places us in a

02:17.800 --> 02:24.160
diplomatic antechamber in a state of what the author calls maximum channel density. It's a great setup.

02:24.260 --> 02:28.980
Yeah. Early morning light, plaster walls. But the author spends significant time describing

02:28.980 --> 02:35.900
what isn't there. Yes. The negative cartography. Exactly. The walls bear these faint discolorations

02:35.900 --> 02:41.600
of old maps and portraits that have been taken down. So the space is literally defined by subtraction.

02:41.700 --> 02:46.220
You can only see the ghosts of the authorities that used to be there. And people in the room

02:46.220 --> 02:49.860
aren't arranged logically. They're just gathered wherever there's physical space.

02:50.060 --> 02:54.040
And the noise, the acoustic environment is critical. It's not deafening,

02:54.340 --> 02:59.640
but it's this continuous equilibrium. Speech has stopped functioning as discrete messages and just

02:59.640 --> 03:04.580
become ambient noise. Just a distributed field of activity. And out of that noise, we get this

03:04.580 --> 03:10.460
micro interaction between two diplomats. And honestly, it serves as the blueprint for the entire human

03:10.460 --> 03:15.140
administrative condition. Well, the road argument. Yeah. So they're having this collegial but

03:15.140 --> 03:22.200
fundamentally adversarial exchange. The first diplomat says the road was clear. And the second

03:22.200 --> 03:28.220
diplomat waits this highly measured interval, like a deliberate pause and replies, that is not what we

03:28.220 --> 03:33.520
were told. Then the first one counters the report you received was compiled yesterday. And the second one

03:33.520 --> 03:40.480
says the report I received was subsequently revised. And that's it. Now, in any rational truth-seeking

03:40.480 --> 03:46.480
paradigm, that conversation has to stop, right? You have to establish the facts. Was the road clear

03:46.480 --> 03:51.820
or not? But they never resolve it. They don't even try. Neither voice rises in pitch. The contradiction

03:51.820 --> 03:57.500
just hangs there. It subsists as a shared premise. They continue to build their exchange right on top of

03:57.500 --> 04:03.240
it. Each statement implicitly grants the prior statement this provisional validity that it hasn't

04:03.240 --> 04:09.340
actually earned. It totally reminds me of a chaotic corporate Slack channel or like a massive email

04:09.340 --> 04:14.560
thread where everyone is talking. No one is actually agreeing on the fundamental facts. But the project

04:14.560 --> 04:19.320
just keeps moving forward anyway. That's a perfect analogy because they're talking directly through the

04:19.320 --> 04:24.780
contradiction, using the disagreement itself to make their next maneuver. But I mean, let me push back

04:24.780 --> 04:29.700
on this a little. Aren't these diplomats technically failing at their jobs if they aren't communicating

04:29.700 --> 04:36.560
facts? Well, if we evaluate them by truth-seeking, sure. But Flyxion argues human institutions aren't

04:36.560 --> 04:41.880
designed to seek truth. They're designed to sustain their own operations. Right. The system just wants

04:41.880 --> 04:47.140
to survive. Exactly. And by that metric, these people are succeeding at the absolute highest level.

04:47.540 --> 04:52.760
Which brings us to Orario, the central character. The perfect bureaucrat. He's a career diplomat,

04:52.760 --> 04:59.480
and the text says he possesses trained eligibility. He's this perfectly functional node. His face is

04:59.480 --> 05:04.900
meticulously conditioned to receive vast amounts of information without reflecting any of it back.

05:05.020 --> 05:10.740
He's totally insulated. He watches the servant carrying a tray of cups, and he notes how the trembling

05:10.740 --> 05:16.440
liquid is basically a physical transcription of the servant's nervousness. And he catalogs all these

05:16.440 --> 05:22.860
unresolved contradictions around him without ever experiencing cognitive overload. Because his

05:22.860 --> 05:28.600
system doesn't require global coherence. It only requires locally adequate responses. Right. He only

05:28.600 --> 05:34.540
needs to execute the next procedurally correct move within his immediate radius. And there's this visual

05:34.540 --> 05:40.680
metaphor the screenplay uses for this that is just brilliant. Orario goes to the central table

05:40.680 --> 05:46.500
covered in documents. The sealed ones and the opened ones. Yeah. So he picks up two documents. One has a

05:46.500 --> 05:52.320
heavy intact wax seal. The other one has already been open, so the creases are soft and worn out.

05:52.640 --> 05:58.920
And he takes the open document, folds it up tightly, and physically slips it inside the sealed document.

05:59.160 --> 06:04.200
He doesn't even fasten it. He just applies slight pressure with his palms to test its coherence.

06:04.320 --> 06:09.580
Right. So on the outside, it looks like this unified, finalized object. It looks resolved.

06:09.580 --> 06:15.620
But inside, it's fundamentally unresolved. The formal closure on the outside is actively enclosing a prior

06:15.620 --> 06:21.340
breach on the inside without ever actually fixing it. It's the material emblem of the entire text.

06:22.020 --> 06:28.580
Institutional logic in a nutshell, formal cohesion, masking deep internal contradiction.

06:29.000 --> 06:34.580
And it just goes back onto the table into circulation. The incoherence is completely absorbed into the

06:34.580 --> 06:39.180
production process. Which is wild. And this idea of unresolved documents,

06:39.180 --> 06:43.960
perfectly transitions us into how human negotiations actually play out in this world.

06:44.100 --> 06:49.300
Right. Because we follow Rorario out of the antechamber and into a private reception chamber.

06:49.300 --> 06:54.240
The vibe totally shifts. The acoustics are deadened. The lighting is deliberate. He's there

06:54.240 --> 06:59.580
to negotiate with a nodal woman over some ecclesiastical jurisdiction in a contested district.

06:59.900 --> 07:03.920
And their interaction is a masterclass in this exact operational functionality we've been

07:03.920 --> 07:08.480
talking about. They don't argue. They perform this highly sophisticated linguistic dance.

07:08.480 --> 07:13.520
Yeah. She listens to the trajectory of his syntax, not just the words. She catches these tiny

07:13.520 --> 07:18.640
modifications and smoothly incorporates them. And over several minutes, they engineer this

07:18.640 --> 07:22.020
incredibly delicate, highly compressed Latin agreement.

07:22.300 --> 07:26.660
A profound, fragile agreement. And then the architecture itself betrays them.

07:26.780 --> 07:26.980
The hud.

07:26.980 --> 07:34.400
Yes. A hidden lateral door opens. And the noble woman's husband walks in. His presence instantly

07:34.400 --> 07:36.380
alters the social geometry of the room.

07:36.520 --> 07:40.980
He just casually drops a bomb. He says the matter has already been referred to a cardinal's office,

07:41.280 --> 07:44.420
so any agreement they make is void. And then he just leaves.

07:44.420 --> 07:46.260
Just exits right back through the same door.

07:46.520 --> 07:50.620
Okay, so wait. If the agreement is instantly dissolved the second he speaks,

07:51.160 --> 07:55.740
why did the noble woman even bother with the linguistic dance? Like, is she just killing time?

07:55.740 --> 08:00.800
Well, no. The act of negotiating itself is the objective. It's an exercise in maintaining the

08:00.800 --> 08:05.860
institutional perimeter. When the husband vaporizes their agreement, she doesn't get mad.

08:06.080 --> 08:08.300
She doesn't even acknowledge that her time was wasted.

08:08.560 --> 08:11.360
Nope. She perfectly pivots. She tells Vororio,

08:11.920 --> 08:16.840
I will send word when the referral has been clarified. It's a flawless operational response.

08:17.300 --> 08:22.220
Treating the absolute destruction of their agreement as just an administrative interval.

08:22.220 --> 08:28.380
Exactly. And Vororio just inclines his head. A gesture of receipt, not agreement. Nothing was

08:28.380 --> 08:33.640
achieved. The district is still contested. But the ambiguity is treated as a provisional closure.

08:34.040 --> 08:39.360
They both get to exit the interaction without breaking the institutional facade. I mean,

08:39.460 --> 08:43.740
substantively, it was totally void. But administratively, huge success.

08:44.100 --> 08:49.780
And we see this principle escalate even further when Vororio moves to the administrative writing room.

08:49.780 --> 08:56.340
Oh, the scene is so stressful. Bare walls, heavy table. Vororio sits there while a clerk

08:56.340 --> 09:01.080
verbally summarizes a long document while he's simultaneously reading the actual document

09:01.080 --> 09:05.340
himself. Right. Taking in two streams of the same information at different cognitive rates.

09:05.920 --> 09:12.040
Flyxion calls it a stereoptic epistemic effect. He's perceiving depth by noticing the difference

09:12.040 --> 09:16.060
between the written word and the spoken summary. And the problem he's trying to solve here is like

09:16.060 --> 09:22.120
a manager's ultimate nightmare. He has two sealed documents on his desk. Both give instructions on

09:22.120 --> 09:27.360
the exact same geopolitical situation. And both come from valid, overlapping authorities. But the

09:27.360 --> 09:33.440
instructions are entirely incompatible. Not directly contradicting, like, attack versus retreat. But

09:33.440 --> 09:38.600
following one inherently violates the logic of the other. There is no synthesis possible in the real

09:38.600 --> 09:43.960
world. So what does a perfectly functioning institutional node do? He doesn't try to synthesize the

09:43.960 --> 09:48.980
real world implications, that's for sure. And he doesn't ask a higher authority for clarification

09:48.980 --> 09:54.920
because that would halt the machinery. So what does he do? He takes a blank sheet of paper and he

09:54.920 --> 10:01.240
graphs a third document. He selectively extracts only the specific semantic elements from the first two

10:01.240 --> 10:06.440
documents that don't conflict with each other. He strips away the context, the intent, the actual

10:06.440 --> 10:11.840
directives, and just harvests the neutral vocabulary. It's wild. It's like a manager getting

10:11.840 --> 10:18.420
conflicting instructions from two bosses and writing a generic memo that satisfies both without actually

10:18.420 --> 10:24.260
solving the problem. Exactly. And the text explicitly states that this third document appears to proceed

10:24.260 --> 10:29.720
from the first two, but it actually proceeds from the space between them. So what does this all mean for

10:29.720 --> 10:35.420
the truth? Like, actual truth? It means the incoherence is absorbed into the production process.

10:35.420 --> 10:41.880
He's created a phantom space that didn't exist in reality. Human institutions prioritize the

10:41.880 --> 10:47.600
appearance of an open functioning system over actual resolution. As long as the paper keeps moving,

10:48.100 --> 10:54.100
the system declares itself healthy, even if it's completely divorced from reality. Which is exactly

10:54.100 --> 11:00.780
why the author violently pulls us out of this paperwork purgatory. Yes. The shift is so jarring.

11:00.780 --> 11:06.320
We move from the silent logic of the writing room straight into the raw sensory environment of a

11:06.320 --> 11:12.560
stable courtyard, the animal domain. Because if human systems are paralyzed by this need for

11:12.560 --> 11:17.700
perfect documentation, the question is, do we actually need all this representational mediation

11:17.700 --> 11:24.600
to survive? Inflation says no by showing us pure biology. Rorario is walking through the courtyard and

11:24.600 --> 11:30.040
sees a farrier working on a massive horse. The farrier has the horse's right foreleg raised,

11:30.040 --> 11:35.080
clamped between his knees, and he's aggressively filing the hoof with a rasp. Think about the

11:35.080 --> 11:41.100
biomechanics here. A massive prey animal balancing on three legs while a predator species uses a metal

11:41.100 --> 11:46.360
tool on its foundation. The cognitive overhead required to maintain that stance is immense.

11:46.540 --> 11:51.760
The text points out how the horse is making thousands of continuous fine-grained micro-adjustments

11:51.760 --> 11:56.880
to its weight. Every time the farrier applies pressure, the horse recalculates its center of gravity.

11:56.880 --> 12:01.800
But there's no executive oversight. The horse doesn't conceptualize a theory of balance.

12:02.200 --> 12:07.500
It just engages in this immediate, proprioceptive negotiation with physical constraint.

12:07.900 --> 12:12.680
The text says there's no hesitation. Not the suppression of hesitation, just its absence.

12:12.880 --> 12:14.060
It's purely responsive.

12:14.720 --> 12:19.960
And Rorario watches this for maybe three seconds. But it's enough for him to realize this animal has a

12:19.960 --> 12:25.020
physical competence his entire administrative apparatus completely lacks.

12:25.020 --> 12:31.060
And we see the human failure of that competence magnified right after this. The map versus the

12:31.060 --> 12:32.020
territory scene.

12:32.480 --> 12:37.760
We're on a ruined, rutted road in post-Mohax, Hungary. The geopolitical landscape is a mess.

12:38.000 --> 12:42.720
Borders have shifted. Markers are destroyed. The map no longer matches the physical territory.

12:43.060 --> 12:47.360
And there's a human rider on this road, and he's completely halted. He's frantically looking back and

12:47.360 --> 12:52.120
forth between his vellum map and the physical landscape. He's completely paralyzed by his document.

12:52.120 --> 12:57.600
But on the exact same road, sharing the same visual field, is a pair of oxen pulling a heavy cart.

12:58.400 --> 13:02.760
They encounter a section of the road that's been washed out, leaving just a narrow band of mud.

13:02.900 --> 13:04.720
Do they consult a topographical survey?

13:04.920 --> 13:05.240
No.

13:05.540 --> 13:09.060
Do they need a historical understanding of why the road is ruined? No.

13:09.700 --> 13:15.980
The late ox just leans slightly left into the yoke, adjusts its pulling force, and keeps moving.

13:16.100 --> 13:21.180
It's distributed adjustment. It happens continuously in real time as the hoof meets the mud.

13:21.180 --> 13:27.520
And then Flixin gives us this beautiful image of a little girl blowing soap bubbles on a village road.

13:28.040 --> 13:30.400
Her dog is relentlessly tracking them.

13:30.560 --> 13:37.860
Throwing its entire body into the air to intercept a bubble, predicting the exact trajectory with total commitment.

13:38.340 --> 13:41.700
It snaps its jaws, the bubble pops, and vanishes.

13:42.040 --> 13:44.660
And the dog just lands and instantly tracks the next one.

13:44.980 --> 13:49.300
It doesn't care that the object vanished. It doesn't experience representational trauma.

13:49.300 --> 13:51.700
Because it's not operating on a theory of the bubble.

13:51.900 --> 13:54.320
Right. It's operating on immediate sensory input.

13:54.480 --> 14:00.820
Let me jump in here, though, because reading this, you might think, okay, are we supposed to think planning and maps are completely useless?

14:01.040 --> 14:03.260
Like, should we all just act like dogs chasing bubbles?

14:03.520 --> 14:07.380
Well, if we connect this to the bigger picture, the text isn't anti-intellectual.

14:07.940 --> 14:10.120
It's contrasting response latency.

14:10.300 --> 14:11.080
Response latency.

14:11.080 --> 14:15.720
Right. The time it takes for a system to process a stimulus and react.

14:16.360 --> 14:22.820
The human world in this story has massive response latency because everything is mediated by representations.

14:23.660 --> 14:25.920
Maps, ledgers, diplomatic reports.

14:26.420 --> 14:30.740
So when the physical reality diverges from the representation, humans just freeze.

14:31.260 --> 14:33.340
The writer freezes because his map is wrong.

14:33.880 --> 14:38.040
The landscape has to be perfectly legible before institutions authorize action.

14:38.040 --> 14:41.980
Exactly. They demand a global coherence the universe won't provide.

14:43.420 --> 14:45.780
But the animals operate on distributed adjustment.

14:46.600 --> 14:50.440
The oxen's hooves touch the mud, and the adjustment is instantaneous.

14:51.020 --> 14:53.600
They don't need a global theory of the road to take the next step.

14:53.740 --> 14:55.720
No. They interact directly with reality.

14:56.460 --> 14:58.280
But this brings up a massive tension.

14:58.920 --> 15:05.620
If humans are tracked by documents and animals are freed by physical immediacy, where do human stories and identities fit in?

15:05.700 --> 15:07.460
Which brings us to the performative domain.

15:07.460 --> 15:09.040
This is where it gets really interesting.

15:09.160 --> 15:13.740
Because if administration fails, order is maintained through reiteration with variation.

15:14.020 --> 15:17.060
Right. We go to a market square in Regensburg to see some street theater.

15:17.200 --> 15:18.500
The biblical sacrifice of Isaac.

15:18.780 --> 15:20.300
The audience knows the story.

15:20.820 --> 15:25.460
Abraham binds Isaac, raises the knife, and at the last second, an angel intervenes.

15:25.460 --> 15:34.620
But Flyxion notes that there are older historical traditions where the angel doesn't arrive in time, where the knife actually falls.

15:35.020 --> 15:35.160
Yeah.

15:35.240 --> 15:39.600
And the audience is sort of unconsciously aware of this instability.

15:39.920 --> 15:43.960
And on this specific day, the actor playing the angel misses his cue.

15:44.240 --> 15:45.580
He is a second and a half late.

15:45.980 --> 15:47.260
In theater time, that's an eternity.

15:47.260 --> 15:48.020
Oh, absolutely.

15:48.540 --> 15:49.700
The prop knife is descending.

15:50.160 --> 15:51.400
The intervention isn't happening.

15:51.820 --> 15:53.840
The audience collectively holds its breath.

15:54.040 --> 15:58.660
Because for those 1.5 seconds, both outcomes are completely plausible.

15:58.940 --> 16:01.240
They're suspended in the potential of alternative history.

16:01.900 --> 16:07.340
When the angel finally stumbles out, the sigh of relief is just the dominant structure reasserting itself.

16:07.420 --> 16:09.040
And then they just reset the props and do it again.

16:09.400 --> 16:11.860
The identity of Isaac is just a structural role.

16:12.020 --> 16:14.100
It entirely detaches from the bearer.

16:14.100 --> 16:17.280
We see this taken to a chaotic extreme in the tavern scene.

16:17.640 --> 16:19.320
Oh, the tavern Goliath.

16:19.820 --> 16:20.540
So good.

16:20.920 --> 16:22.780
Two men stand on a tavern table.

16:23.300 --> 16:24.560
One is physically massive.

16:24.900 --> 16:25.760
The other is slight.

16:26.340 --> 16:29.140
The massive guy declares, I am Goliath.

16:29.300 --> 16:31.600
The smaller one says, I am David.

16:31.740 --> 16:33.700
Someone tosses David a makeshift sling.

16:33.820 --> 16:35.320
He mimes throwing a stone.

16:35.820 --> 16:39.780
And the giant falls to the floor with this exaggerated theatricality.

16:39.860 --> 16:40.720
A crowd cheers.

16:41.200 --> 16:42.040
Narrative fulfilled.

16:42.040 --> 16:46.040
But then, they get up, and a new patron steps onto the table.

16:46.580 --> 16:50.940
The massive guy who just played Goliath proudly declares, I am David.

16:51.200 --> 16:53.700
And the average-sized new guy says, I'm in Goliath.

16:53.740 --> 16:55.040
They literally swap roles.

16:55.400 --> 16:58.380
The identities have completely detached from their physical attributes.

16:58.500 --> 16:59.540
And the crowd doesn't care.

16:59.660 --> 17:07.300
It reaches this point of farcical brilliance when two men step up at the same time, look at each other, and both stubbornly declare, I am David.

17:07.300 --> 17:09.240
The system is momentarily jammed.

17:09.780 --> 17:12.480
Two identical signifiers competing for one space.

17:12.720 --> 17:15.380
And someone in the back just yells, then one must be Goliath.

17:15.460 --> 17:16.080
And they shrug.

17:16.440 --> 17:18.300
And one of them just accepts the role of the giant.

17:18.620 --> 17:23.240
It's like playing a video game where you die and respawn, or seeing different actors play Batman.

17:23.240 --> 17:32.020
The audience accepts that the system functions perfectly well, even when the official narrative and the enacted reality are totally misaligned.

17:32.220 --> 17:33.920
Contradiction does not entail breakdown.

17:34.440 --> 17:37.680
We see this synthesized perfectly in the puppet theater scene.

17:37.780 --> 17:38.000
Right.

17:38.120 --> 17:39.400
The story of Cain and Abel.

17:39.400 --> 17:43.960
So there's a human narrator outside the booth, and the wooden puppet's inside.

17:44.800 --> 17:49.180
The narrator says, heavily, the elder brother brought the offering that was not accepted.

17:49.380 --> 17:57.380
But inside the booth, the physical wooden puppet of Cain places his miniature offering on the altar, and it sits there perfectly stable.

17:57.660 --> 17:59.380
It is physically accepted by the set.

17:59.680 --> 18:02.840
Then the narrator says, the younger brought that which was favored.

18:03.400 --> 18:08.920
But the puppeteer fumbles, and Abel's offering slides off the slanted altar and clatters to the floor.

18:08.920 --> 18:13.980
The narration and the physical reality are directly, undeniably contradicting each other.

18:14.400 --> 18:15.580
The voice says success.

18:15.980 --> 18:17.920
The physical object demonstrates failure.

18:18.200 --> 18:19.820
Yet the audience doesn't riot.

18:20.280 --> 18:21.300
They don't demand a refund.

18:21.700 --> 18:24.200
The performance just continues uninterrupted.

18:24.660 --> 18:28.520
This totally prepares the listener for the ultimate failure of the archive.

18:28.660 --> 18:29.360
Oh, okay, yes.

18:29.800 --> 18:35.060
Because we've seen that human documents are flawed, and human performances are contradictory.

18:35.060 --> 18:40.220
So what happens when these flawed documents are stored and copied over decades?

18:40.680 --> 18:45.700
We enter the architecture of epistemological decay, starting at the Diet of Regensburg.

18:45.920 --> 18:55.120
This massive assembly chamber where elite theological delegates are having this endless, desperate argument over a single clause in a theological book.

18:55.120 --> 18:57.060
They are trapped in semantic creep.

18:57.640 --> 19:01.740
Every time one faction tries to make a noun more precise, it offends another tradition.

19:01.900 --> 19:02.020
Yeah.

19:02.240 --> 19:05.800
So they add an amendment, which introduces a new, contested term.

19:05.980 --> 19:09.380
The document just swells and grows heavier without ever getting closer to the truth.

19:09.680 --> 19:11.040
While up in the window frame...

19:11.040 --> 19:11.680
Yes, the spider.

19:11.680 --> 19:15.560
A spider is quietly, efficiently building a perfect web.

19:16.000 --> 19:16.960
It doesn't hold committees.

19:17.280 --> 19:19.700
It just moves from anchor point to anchor point.

19:20.340 --> 19:26.320
By the afternoon, the web is mathematically perfect, while the human document is still a catastrophic mess.

19:26.680 --> 19:30.400
But the true violence of the archive happens in the quiet moments of preservation.

19:31.820 --> 19:33.580
Look at the cartographer scene.

19:33.720 --> 19:37.580
He's copying an old, heavily revised map onto fresh vellum.

19:37.580 --> 19:42.740
The original has faint, erased lines and wobbly, ambiguous borders.

19:43.120 --> 19:44.820
You can see the history of the disputes.

19:45.240 --> 19:47.860
It's messy, but it's deeply historically true.

19:48.320 --> 19:51.040
But the cartographer doesn't replicate the ambiguity.

19:51.600 --> 19:55.500
Where the original is wobbly, he draws a sharp, clean, confident line.

19:55.540 --> 19:57.940
He literally erases the history of the revisions.

19:58.120 --> 19:58.480
Exactly.

19:58.880 --> 20:02.320
He prioritizes aesthetic cleanliness over historical truth.

20:02.660 --> 20:05.700
And this raises an important question about history itself.

20:05.700 --> 20:11.100
Every time a human system copies something, it resolves ambiguity into false certainty.

20:11.220 --> 20:14.860
So you're saying the cleanest, most confident map is actually the biggest lie.

20:15.420 --> 20:15.860
Pretty much.

20:16.040 --> 20:16.200
Yeah.

20:16.300 --> 20:19.680
And this paralysis hits Rorario in the Library of Contradictions.

20:20.140 --> 20:23.040
He finds three documents describing the exact same event.

20:23.640 --> 20:27.320
Document 1 and 2 completely contradict each other based on the author's perspectives.

20:27.580 --> 20:31.880
And Document 3 introduces a totally different account written by someone who wasn't even there.

20:32.180 --> 20:32.420
Right.

20:32.560 --> 20:33.680
The archive worked perfectly.

20:33.680 --> 20:37.740
It preserved the documents, but it made the truth impossible to find.

20:38.060 --> 20:42.560
Which sets up the absolute masterpiece of this section, the five-document sequence.

20:43.020 --> 20:48.360
Rorario lines up five documents chronologically to trace how a phantom law is born.

20:48.480 --> 20:48.620
Right.

20:48.660 --> 20:50.320
So Document 1 is the original text.

20:50.560 --> 20:55.700
It's incredibly dense, highly conditional, transcribed by a scribe who was in the room.

20:56.080 --> 20:58.880
Accurate, but a massive cognitive burden to read.

20:59.040 --> 21:01.660
So Document 2 is a summary made eight months later.

21:01.660 --> 21:05.180
The guy wants to save space, so he drops the conditional forces.

21:06.000 --> 21:10.660
He inadvertently turns a nuanced if-then agreement into an absolute rule.

21:11.040 --> 21:19.320
Then Document 3 is written by someone in a different jurisdiction who cites the summary as if it were the absolute truth, constructing this sweeping legal interpretation.

21:19.320 --> 21:22.700
Then Document 4, a translation of the third document.

21:23.200 --> 21:25.320
The translator subtly alters a legal term.

21:25.700 --> 21:28.540
They translate a placed boundary as a recognized boundary.

21:28.700 --> 21:31.780
Shifting it from a physical reality to a psychological consensus.

21:32.000 --> 21:32.340
Exactly.

21:32.740 --> 21:35.660
And finally, Document 5, the one currently in use.

21:35.880 --> 21:40.200
It contains a highly specific legal phrase that literally no one ever wrote.

21:40.200 --> 21:44.600
It was autonomously generated by the friction of the transmission chain itself.

21:45.200 --> 21:48.440
The errors mutated and manifested a law out of the ether.

21:48.700 --> 21:51.180
And the official legal rule was written by nobody.

21:51.320 --> 21:52.120
Written by nobody.

21:52.320 --> 21:55.320
But it's now generating real geopolitical obligations.

21:56.200 --> 21:57.980
The archive doesn't preserve the event.

21:58.340 --> 22:02.860
It preserves the residue of the institutional processes that crush the event.

22:02.860 --> 22:09.440
Spending decades of your life processing this confidently false administrative noise, it takes a toll.

22:10.260 --> 22:12.160
Rororio reaches his absolute limit.

22:12.400 --> 22:14.440
He hits what Flyxion calls Seta 3rd.

22:14.520 --> 22:14.740
Right.

22:15.000 --> 22:16.720
Saturation and the breakdown of meaning.

22:17.160 --> 22:21.380
We find him in his study at night, drafting a complex administrative sentence.

22:21.620 --> 22:23.860
He writes the greed aims, the subordinate clauses.

22:24.340 --> 22:25.940
He gets to the central claim.

22:26.020 --> 22:27.020
And his pen stops.

22:27.160 --> 22:27.660
It just stops.

22:27.860 --> 22:30.580
Not because he's out of ink, but because the continuation hasn't arrived.

22:30.580 --> 22:33.480
He's reached the terminus of his representational framework.

22:33.680 --> 22:36.260
He holds the pen there so long an inkblot falls.

22:36.640 --> 22:40.560
Then he calmly folds the incomplete letter, seals it, and sends it anyway.

22:40.840 --> 22:44.280
And then a woman visits him, an old friend or relative.

22:44.920 --> 22:48.160
She speaks warmly about family, about normal human existence.

22:48.580 --> 22:52.960
And as she speaks, Rororio experiences this terrifying perceptual shift.

22:53.320 --> 22:55.780
He hears the prosodic features of her voice.

22:56.200 --> 22:56.980
The pitch.

22:57.560 --> 22:58.220
The cadence.

22:58.220 --> 23:00.800
He parses the grammar perfectly.

23:01.520 --> 23:04.600
But the semantic meaning completely vanishes.

23:04.800 --> 23:06.280
Reception without elaboration.

23:06.420 --> 23:07.820
It's like semantic satiation.

23:08.060 --> 23:10.680
You know, when you say a word so many times, it loses all meaning.

23:11.020 --> 23:12.600
But applied to his entire life.

23:12.820 --> 23:14.240
The tragedy here is profound.

23:15.180 --> 23:16.560
The system has reached its limit.

23:17.360 --> 23:20.200
Language no longer functions as a tool of negotiation for him.

23:20.500 --> 23:22.980
It's just the residue of eliminated possibilities.

23:23.220 --> 23:26.560
And while he's collapsing, the natural world just keeps existing indifferently.

23:26.560 --> 23:32.360
There's this image of a succulent plant growing out of a crack in a pristine administrative corridor.

23:32.720 --> 23:37.360
It brushes against the sleeves of passing clerks, leaving green stains on official documents.

23:37.620 --> 23:39.400
Moths endlessly orbit a flame.

23:39.560 --> 23:43.300
A massive swarm of birds perfectly coordinates its flight without a central leader.

23:43.740 --> 23:48.680
The animal world persists effortlessly, while the human world collapses under the weight of its own meaning.

23:48.680 --> 23:51.140
Which transitions us to the final coda.

23:51.840 --> 23:55.720
We jump from the 16th century to Amsterdam in the late 17th century.

23:56.500 --> 24:00.760
The chaos of Rorio's life is about to be processed by the printing press.

24:01.260 --> 24:05.820
A woman in a printing house carries the messy, handwritten manuscript of Rorio's life.

24:06.320 --> 24:11.820
She glances at it for exactly 12 seconds, processing without retention, and drops it on a desk.

24:12.060 --> 24:14.040
And the compositor sets the type.

24:14.400 --> 24:16.300
He sees two variant words in the handwriting.

24:16.560 --> 24:17.280
He doesn't deliberate.

24:17.280 --> 24:18.320
He just picks one.

24:18.620 --> 24:22.940
The alternative reality is physically dropped back into the type case, just gone forever.

24:23.220 --> 24:25.000
Erased by the mechanics of reproduction.

24:25.880 --> 24:27.780
Which leads us to Pierre Bale.

24:28.100 --> 24:29.580
Rotterdam, 1697.

24:29.920 --> 24:31.700
He's compiling his famous dictionary.

24:32.180 --> 24:34.860
He has three contradictory sources about Rorio.

24:35.040 --> 24:38.780
And he efficiently discards the unverifiable, the messy, the contradictory.

24:39.440 --> 24:43.540
He reduces Rorio's entire agonizing complex existence.

24:43.540 --> 24:46.600
The uncrossed bridges, the shifting maps, the stopped pen.

24:46.660 --> 24:49.960
All of it into exactly 11 tidy lines of text.

24:50.360 --> 24:51.520
And he closes the book.

24:52.460 --> 25:01.600
Outside, Rotterdam goes about its business, completely indifferent to the fact that a man's complex reality has just been permanently erased into a neat encyclopedia entry.

25:01.600 --> 25:05.580
And the final line of the screenplay is just a gut punch.

25:05.760 --> 25:07.160
Nothing appears to have been lost.

25:07.260 --> 25:08.160
Oh, man.

25:08.420 --> 25:14.380
The ultimate success of the administrative system is the total erasure of the actual messy truth.

25:14.520 --> 25:15.380
It brings it all home.

25:15.640 --> 25:19.160
The encyclopedia entry is entirely successful within its own constraints.

25:19.580 --> 25:20.440
It's stable.

25:20.700 --> 25:21.580
It's legible.

25:21.580 --> 25:23.660
But it doesn't know what produced it.

25:24.140 --> 25:26.160
Humans build systems to capture the world.

25:26.380 --> 25:28.680
But the systems only end up capturing themselves.

25:29.180 --> 25:30.140
What an incredible journey.

25:30.620 --> 25:39.520
From the noisy diplomatic antechambers to the intuitive animal world, through the eroding archives, and finally to Pierre Bale's 11 lines of sanitized history.

25:39.680 --> 25:42.880
It really forces you to re-evaluate how we process information.

25:43.160 --> 25:43.720
It does.

25:43.720 --> 25:50.680
And I want you, the listener, to think about your own digital footprint, your emails, your social media posts, your official records.

25:51.140 --> 25:57.580
Hundreds of years from now, some future archivist or algorithm will compile your life into the equivalent of Bale's 11 lines.

25:57.740 --> 25:59.440
They'll smooth out all your contradictions.

25:59.580 --> 25:59.820
Exactly.

26:00.020 --> 26:07.840
They'll discard your messy realities and present a perfectly clean, totally logical, and fundamentally untrue version of who you were.

26:08.480 --> 26:12.880
What ghost of your actual life will be left completely unrecorded on the compositor's floor?

26:12.880 --> 26:14.100
Something to think about.

26:14.520 --> 26:15.360
We'll see you next time.

